A C# `struct` named `Parcel` has two fields: `int Weight` and `List<string> Labels`.

```csharp
var first = new Parcel
{
    Weight = 5,
    Labels = new List<string> { "fragile" }
};
var second = first;
second.Weight = 8;
second.Labels.Add("express");
second.Labels = new List<string> { "returned" };
```

Afterwards, `first.Weight` is still `5`, and `first.Labels` contains `"fragile"` and `"express"`. Explain why changing the weight, adding a label, and replacing the list have these different effects. State what `second = first` copies.

A C# `class` named `Meter` has a mutable `int Reading` field.

```csharp
var primary = new Meter { Reading = 10 };
var backup = primary;
backup.Reading = 25;
backup = new Meter { Reading = 0 };
```

The field change makes `primary.Reading` become `25`, but the final assignment leaves it at `25`. Explain why. Include what `backup = primary` copies and what the final assignment changes.
